# Corrections loglan-update makes to the originals when it copies them into
# ~/.loglan/src.  The originals here, and the ones it downloads, are never
# changed themselves.
#
# One correction per line, tab-separated:
#
#     path<TAB>text as published<TAB>text to put in its place
#
# The path is as in MANIFEST.  Every occurrence is replaced; \t and \n stand
# for a tab and a newline, \\ for a backslash.  A correction whose text is no
# longer found -- fixed upstream, perhaps -- is reported and otherwise
# ignored.

# hadja hadje hadji hadjo: a composite of C ya syi ya (亞細亞), J Ajiya and E Asia,
# coined as adja and given its h- when vowel-initial predicates went; the scores
# were never filled in (0/0) and are left so.  The Chinese is spelled "si" where
# the dictionary's own romanization has "syi".
Dictionary/L-to-E-TDR.html	&lt;0/0C ya si ya;	&lt;0/0C ya syi ya;

# toi toa tio tao, as Loglan 1 section 4.3 has them: like ti and ta, the i-word
# is "this", the nearer, and the a-word "that", the earlier.  toi is the remark
# just made ("Toi tradu, That's true (literally, this is true)"), toa an earlier
# one ("Toa falji, that earlier claim was false"); tio the situation just
# alluded to, tao one alluded to earlier.  The Loglan Institute's list of the
# little words (loglan.org, Misc/loglan-lw-by-word.html) agrees: toi "the latter
# remark", toa "the former", tio "the latter situation", tao "the former".  The
# dictionary had toi and toa the other way round, with toa a remark about to be
# made, and tao a second situation rather than an earlier one; and tio's and
# tao's little-word blocks had each other's origin, tao's <ta(rg)o> under tio and
# tio's <ti(dj)o> under tao (Loglan 4 and 5 has <tidjo> for tio, <targo> for tao).
Dictionary/L-to-E-TDR.html	<em class="origin">&lt;ti(dj)o&gt;</em>&nbsp;LW L4 1975 1.0<br>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;(p) <em class="key">that</em>,  a second <em class="key">situation</em> <em class="key">referred to</em> (when tio is already assigned a referent) DA.	<em class="origin">&lt;ta(rg)o&gt;</em>&nbsp;LW L4 1975 1.0<br>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;(p) <em class="key">that</em> <em class="key">situation</em> (DA).
Dictionary/L-to-E-TDR.html	<em class="origin">&lt;ta(rg)o&gt;</em>&nbsp;LW L4 1975 1.0<br>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;(p) <em class="key">this</em>, the <em class="key">situation</em> we are taking about (DA).	<em class="origin">&lt;ti(dj)o&gt;</em>&nbsp;LW L4 1975 1.0<br>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;(p) <em class="key">this</em> <em class="key">situation</em> (DA).
Dictionary/L-to-E-TDR.html	(p) <em class="key">this</em>, the about to be mentioned <em class="key">remark</em>/<em class="key">utterance</em>/<em class="key">textual item</em>. (DA).	(p) <em class="key">that</em> <em class="key">remark</em> (DA).
Dictionary/L-to-E-TDR.html	(p) <em class="key">that</em>, the last mentioned <em class="key">remark</em>/<em class="key">utterance</em>/<em class="key">textual item</em> (DA).	(p) <em class="key">this</em> <em class="key">remark</em> (DA).
